--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
3. WHAT A BUFFER COSTS: SWEEPING THE COMPUTE PER OBJECTFIFO BUFFER
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The calibration above dispatches one buffer, so the lock wait it sees is the cost of getting
data to a core once, unamortised. A real kernel streams: while the core works on buffer i the
DMA fetches i+1. This sweeps the compute per buffer with 16 buffers in flight and watches what
the lock wait does. Same kernel, same core, one xclbin for every point -- only the per-buffer
loop count changes, carried in each buffer's own header.

== pmu_probe [pmu] mode=vector target=4096 trace_size=65536
   events: 0:INSTR_EVENT_0, 1:INSTR_EVENT_1, 2:ACTIVE, 3:MEMORY_STALL, 4:STREAM_STALL, 5:LOCK_STALL, 6:CASCADE_STALL, 7:DISABLED

-----------------------------
[003d:00:01.1] : NPU Phoenix
-----------------------------
Platform
  Name                   : NPU Phoenix 
  Power Mode             : Default 
  Total Columns          : 5 

Estimated Power          : N/A
pyxrt device.get_info(max_clock_frequency_mhz) = 800

PIPELINE SWEEP -- 16 buffers of 64 int32 through one core, vector loop

  cyc/buf     active       lock    issuing  lock %  issue/buf    hw us
----------------------------------------------------------------------
       32      19357       7523      11834    38.9        740    325.5
      128      25253      12059      13192    47.8        824    322.2
      512      31581      11912      19668    37.7       1229    266.0
     2048      56120      11876      44244    21.2       2765    351.0
     8192     149498       6948     142548     4.6       8909    365.0
    32768     547938      12173     535764     2.2      33485    566.6
   131072    2118464       9835    2108628     0.5     131789   1497.6

Lock wait over a 4096x range of compute per buffer: min 6948, max 12173, median 11876 cycles -- flat, not proportional.
Issuing cycles per buffer minus compute: 708, 696, 717, 717, 717, 717, 717
  -> per-buffer overhead is constant at about 717 cycles, independent of the work in the buffer.

READING, and this is the reusable part. Two constants fall out and both are flat.

The lock wait does not scale with the work. Across a 4,096x range of compute per buffer it
stays in the low thousands to low ten-thousands of cycles with no trend, and it varies as much
between repeats of the same point as it does across the sweep, because it is DMA timing. It is
a fixed cost of the dispatch, order 10,000 cycles or 5.6 us at 1.80 GHz, not a per-buffer or
per-byte cost.

The issuing cycles per buffer are the compute plus a constant 717 cycles, at every point in the
sweep. That is not a fit or a trend; the residual is 717 at 131,072 cycles of compute and 717 at
512, four thousand times smaller. The large points are also bit-identical between runs, so the
issuing side of this instrument is deterministic while the lock side is not. That 717
decomposes against numbers already measured here: 512 cycles are this probe kernel's own flush
loop, the 256 event pairs it emits so the trace packet reaches host memory (8 bundles per 4
pairs, results/aie/aie2_isa_static.log), and 190 to 198 cycles are the kernel prologue and
epilogue the section above isolated. The genuine per-buffer handoff -- acquire, release, and
the call -- is what is left, about 15 cycles.

So for a kernel shaped like this one, on one core:

    cycles = n_buffers x (compute_per_buffer + handoff) + fixed_dispatch_lock_wait
    handoff ~ 205 cycles including the kernel call, ~15 of it the ObjectFifo acquire/release
    fixed_dispatch_lock_wait ~ 10,000 cycles (5.6 us at 1.80 GHz)

which says a buffer carrying less than a few hundred cycles of work is mostly handoff, and a
dispatch carrying less than about 10,000 cycles of total work is mostly waiting. Those are
lower bounds for the easiest possible kernel: one core, no cascade, no neighbour traffic, and
operands already in registers.

The hw microseconds column is wall time for the whole dispatch and is NOT the core's view; it
includes the host and DMA costs that results/aie/dispatch_floor_npu.log measured separately.
Two warm-up dispatches precede the sweep, without which the first points are dominated by JIT
and cache warming and read backwards against the work done.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
4. WHAT THE NUMBERS SAY
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Cycles per iteration converge on the measured values from above as the loop grows -- 2.0051 ->
2.0013 -> 2.0003 and 9.0020 -> 9.0005 -> 9.0001 -- because the fixed loop-entry cost amortises.
The instrument reproduces both of S0's loops.

The accounting is the stronger result. Subtracting the traced stall cycles and the loop's own
cycles from the cycles the core was alive leaves EXACTLY 190 cycles on every vector run and
EXACTLY 198 on every scalar run, across a 16x range of work. A residual that is constant rather
than proportional is the kernel's prologue and epilogue, and it is what says the decomposition

    cycles the core is alive = issuing + memory + stream + lock + cascade stalls

closes on this hardware. ACTIVE is inclusive of stall cycles, not exclusive of them: a core
waiting on a lock is still enabled and not halted, so issuing cycles are what remains after the
stalls are subtracted rather than something the hardware reports directly.

LOCK_STALL is the only non-zero stall term in any of these runs, and it is large. The core waits
roughly 8,500 to 12,700 cycles per dispatch on the input ObjectFifo's lock -- about 5 to 7
microseconds at 1.80 GHz -- and that figure barely moves as the loop grows from 4,096 to 65,536
iterations, so it is a fixed cost of getting data to the core and not a function of the work.
On the shortest run it is 18,926 cycles against 8,915 cycles of issuing: the core spends 68% of
its life waiting and 32% computing, on a kernel whose inner loop the disassembly rates as
perfectly scheduled.

That is the mechanism this repo has been inferring from throughput fits and can now measure. It
also sharpens results/aie/dispatch_floor_npu.log's 169.8 microsecond hardware floor: some of
that floor is visible from inside the core as lock wait, and the rest is outside the core
entirely, because 10,000 cycles is only about 3% of 169.8 microseconds.

MEMORY_STALL, STREAM_STALL and CASCADE_STALL are zero throughout, which is correct for this
design -- it has one core, no cascade, no streams beyond the two ObjectFifos, and a loop whose
operands are in registers. They are not shown to work by this run. A kernel that uses them is
needed before any of those three can be trusted, and that is the next step.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
WHAT THIS DOES NOT SHOW
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- Only LOCK_STALL has been seen non-zero. Three of the four stall categories are unexercised.
- One core tile, logical Tile(0,2), physical (2,1). One power mode.
- The traced window starts when the trace unit is enabled, not when the dispatch begins, so the
  cycles the core was alive are not the whole submit-to-wait bracket and should not be compared
  to it directly.
- ACTIVE being inclusive of stalls is inferred from the accounting closing, not from a document.
- The 8-event budget per tile is a hardware limit; a wider taxonomy needs more than one run.
